    {
      "heading": "A well-designed word problem: a bouncing ball",
      "questions": [
        {
          "q": "This question has three parts. A ball is dropped from a height of 20 meters. Each time it bounces, it rises to 60% of its previous height. (a) Find the height of the ball's rebound after its 4th bounce. (b) Write an expression for the total vertical distance traveled by the ball as it bounces infinitely many times, and evaluate it. (c) After which bounce does the rebound height first fall below 1 meter?",
          "answer": "(a) The rebound height after the $n$th bounce is $h_n=20(0.6)^n$. $h_4=20(0.6)^4=20(0.1296)=2.592$ meters. (b) The ball falls 20 m initially, then for each bounce it rises and falls the same rebound height. Total distance $=20+2\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}20(0.6)^n=20+2\\left(\\dfrac{12}{1-0.6}\\right)=20+2(30)=80$ meters. (c) We need $20(0.6)^n<1\\Rightarrow(0.6)^n<0.05$. Checking: $(0.6)^5=0.0778$ gives $h_5=1.555$ m (still above 1 m), while $(0.6)^6=0.0467$ gives $h_6=0.933$ m (below 1 m). The rebound height first falls below 1 meter after the 6th bounce."
        }
      ]
    },
